    ("v582_density_dominance", "PRIME.DENSITYDOM.01: the quantitative occupation question answered at its first level -- THE DOMINANCE IS DENSITY-DRIVEN (5 checks, ~2 s; discovery probe density_dominance_probe.py 5/5, verdict DENSITY-DRIVEN).  [MEASURED, central] the smoothed comb (moving average, declared windows 2%/5%/10%/20% of the lag range) reproduces det S through the exact lag-side identity on every regular window: 0.998-0.999 at 2% and 0.987-0.993 at 5% -- the dominance det S >> det B is carried by the deterministic density PROFILE; the prime fluctuations contribute ~1% at the det-S-relevant scales; the ratio degrades smoothly to 0.81-0.89 at 20% (density features down to ~5% of the lag range carry it).  [MEASURED, decisive control] on the SCRAMBLED window the smooth model captures only 0.70: random placement has genuine fluctuation content, the REAL comb is anomalously SMOOTH at these scales -- the arithmetic-placement content (v573: det S suppressed ~50x below random) is the deterministic density profile, not fine noise.  [MEASURED, honest boundary] the one anomalous window (h = 1292, the P <= 0 window of v570) deviates (1.24 at 5%): at the sieve horizon the fluctuation part is not negligible; the reduction is a statement about the regular windows.  [C] THE RELOCATION: the v570 h-uniform dominance question reduces -- up to a measured ~1% fluctuation correction needing only a CRUDE bound -- to a positivity/growth statement about the SMOOTH density functional: PNT-level regularity, not prime fluctuations; the named next step is a closed-form bound on the smooth functional; no uniformity, no rate, NO RH statement; Problem 7.1 untouched.  Python-only, counted per GATE.WOLFRAM.02.  Discovery: experiments/tfpt-discovery/density_dominance_probe.py (2026-07-31, 5/5, DENSITY-DRIVEN)."),
